#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "a4eeb5c3-c1d5-5eb2-bb4e-03de915e0dab" :question/math-academy-id "e-8579" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle   = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(1)(0)+(3)(k-1)+(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b2d31046-ad1b-5de2-8d2b-19c2ce8b2a0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8580" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "a9ae1458-cfaf-5a3d-a1c8-d7d539480fd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8581" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot k)+1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6k-2-4 \\\\  & =6k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k-6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "f084687c-bbe3-5506-b803-ae2c7e450a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8582" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8232307-82a4-53de-bad1-9c5b618328ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308865} {:answer/id #uuid "3aedfa59-331f-5b35-b154-846f12ea70d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308866} {:answer/id #uuid "2e02c947-d75f-5244-ab5f-91bb20564642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308867} {:answer/id #uuid "a46a93a5-b2c7-5738-ab86-46da85f50f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} {:answer/id #uuid "d330537c-aaaf-59b8-8c4a-6fb16adf3eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46a93a5-b2c7-5738-ab86-46da85f50f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} :answer-field/id #uuid "78235b23-fddb-536a-83c9-bb59105b98b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ccbba1a-31b9-5014-bee2-85dad03abf72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140263" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1\\cdot (-2))+4\\cdot (1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-4+8+4k \\\\  & =4+4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4+4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =-4 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41890742-709a-5aca-a2cf-296dc6d9ea7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308872} {:answer/id #uuid "6e49382a-59f6-577b-891b-a93cc6ca46ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308873} {:answer/id #uuid "d9804076-604a-550c-9aaf-e6f1d3abf5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308874} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9f77ac-125a-552b-a163-88574b93b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308875} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef30669-6734-5a93-a3fe-130ce4db12a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9f77ac-125a-552b-a163-88574b93b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308875} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e07f48-c283-5f23-84c8-e79f428261e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8723dbf-c27e-503d-97d5-3da3c89aad5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140265" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1+{t}^{2}$ and $q(t)=-5{t}^{2}+2t+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2)(k-7)+(1)(k)+(2)(k-3) \\\\  & =2k-14+k+2k-6 \\\\  & =5k-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5k-20 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =20 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a3497f-aaf8-5653-893a-4493a6ff5f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308879} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e12f55-8ceb-5032-a428-ab7ec147bc99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308880} {:answer/id #uuid "369b0f62-2779-50d5-b1fd-48c471c80415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308881} {:answer/id #uuid "aa524d18-bec7-509c-a367-f87751658ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308882} {:answer/id #uuid "40910062-0c54-5d32-bdab-d2068f95f684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa524d18-bec7-509c-a367-f87751658ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308882} :answer-field/id #uuid "549b46f4-208d-52d8-8905-a6cf8c846898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34faa34c-2b76-5e17-a69c-2a35512d726e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140266" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=2t+4$ and $q(t)=kt+2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2)(-k+2)+(6)(k+2) \\\\  & =-2k+4+6k+12 \\\\  & =4k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4k+16 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61579f7a-6f95-50ba-9f99-7cf5a13bbf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308886} {:answer/id #uuid "e219778a-7eb5-5706-8b26-9e32b4c02309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308887} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd25c22-538c-5602-b649-0696bcae870d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308888} {:answer/id #uuid "48e7f959-822b-58b1-a5e9-8b80dd475353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308889} {:answer/id #uuid "9706f452-8790-5db5-a1c8-f595acc5e682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e219778a-7eb5-5706-8b26-9e32b4c02309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308887} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8e4176-fb66-5003-96d9-81183258db00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d512d010-6725-5b0e-a4aa-10b8b5ae97d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140267" :question/problem "Let $V=C[1,e]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,e],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{e}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)={ln}^{2}⁡(t)+k$ and $y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}({ln}^{2}⁡(t)+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡(t)}{t}+\\frac{k}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡(t)}{3}+k\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c48e522-2a81-507f-a4cd-e917d911ef4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308893} {:answer/id #uuid "19972557-17d4-5684-8792-06aa818f5b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308894} {:answer/id #uuid "76a25f2c-35e8-5013-8e72-f4cc742a2a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308895} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ac471e-abee-5899-b59e-b11e3a99f3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308896} {:answer/id #uuid "b067ca6a-010b-5422-b551-a6907a2d5e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c48e522-2a81-507f-a4cd-e917d911ef4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308893} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb048b62-903d-5d35-9af3-2b8cbb29a710" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d255fe9-51ef-5ae2-a58c-3387fe7ae572" :question/math-academy-id "q-140434" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}$ and $g(t)=3{t}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{4}$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u={t}^{3}+t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{3}+t+1)^{3}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{4}]-[\\frac{1}{4}(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} {:answer/id #uuid "d0080e9c-95d4-5ed3-af87-271cc9f3c19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132592} {:answer/id #uuid "09b8a0a9-e693-5862-90fd-17e1cab9583d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132593} {:answer/id #uuid "2db798ed-de8d-5f4e-8199-0378fc05ffd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132594} {:answer/id #uuid "53611b71-8da8-53bd-9ff8-777848081934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0843c47f-c932-5f31-8986-c111c47d29f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d41fa515-e181-5501-be57-d1516f0b8643" :question/math-academy-id "q-43979" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)={t}^{2}+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t({t}^{2}+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ef3f295-ffdd-5171-9073-c4d4fda1c706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132603} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5425db-368f-51c9-858f-fb6805ea4287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132604} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc73075-1db6-5d8f-af4b-c8e614f193c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132605} {:answer/id #uuid "3be0dd3f-53cd-5faa-a350-017f118a936d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132606} {:answer/id #uuid "74839eab-be55-5a1e-baa7-ebf21cce9892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74839eab-be55-5a1e-baa7-ebf21cce9892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132607} :answer-field/id #uuid "23a010ec-1293-54ea-92cb-4a0d32734b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51bb8df2-4e37-58ba-9ead-0159a1efcc4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-44057" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1\\cdot k)+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-2-2k+18 \\\\  & =16-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 16-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2c2cc5-066e-586b-913e-9616e3b08805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308900} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e3b27-cb43-5239-9c1d-de600b14c135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} {:answer/id #uuid "13f15a45-f942-5440-8896-310216bebc48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308902} {:answer/id #uuid "5b76f3cd-6843-5530-aceb-701171cf9ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308903} {:answer/id #uuid "d4566944-231f-5940-a100-61d5f7d0fd49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e3b27-cb43-5239-9c1d-de600b14c135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} :answer-field/id #uuid "9277c21b-3673-537b-9988-c4808290f327" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02e28179-afce-5ef7-836d-53202828246c" :question/math-academy-id "q-44058" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca9ba546-f3a6-51cc-8737-8f3f19617c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308907} {:answer/id #uuid "910c263f-ff2f-5af8-8446-b51f85c293f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308908} {:answer/id #uuid "74cccbf1-6dd3-5501-ac01-a7f9096ec3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308909} {:answer/id #uuid "916431d2-23b0-57f5-a625-d6cb0f586704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308910} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5a308b-352f-5159-af84-b7b395c6e06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "910c263f-ff2f-5af8-8446-b51f85c293f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308908} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff52fd6b-249e-5add-9a46-53ec218a998e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3ac8baa-3ca8-5d1d-a2e1-b87529351ba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-44069" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=k-2{t}^{3}$ and $y(t)=t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product is equal to zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(k-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9eb6ac-fad9-5bd1-bf8d-ffbceb4bb169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308914} {:answer/id #uuid "012b9890-2e75-5d46-87dd-9086f13bba15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308915} {:answer/id #uuid "8259007d-4ac8-5f97-95af-dc2a81a0510c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308916} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdbbdc9-8844-5026-83ec-de57ac71dd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308917} {:answer/id #uuid "824cedf0-acf8-53e3-8e9a-06cbf01e9c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "012b9890-2e75-5d46-87dd-9086f13bba15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308915} :answer-field/id #uuid "00b43f98-44c7-5e25-a2ad-4c77c908185a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a65eef03-d081-5848-9425-7aafbf449279" :question/math-academy-id "q-97347" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (2\\cdot (-2))+k\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3-16+k \\\\  & =k-19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ k-19 & =0 \\\\ k & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe75b42-00a2-5bb0-900b-6dd1cac45785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308921} {:answer/id #uuid "1576000c-cf8b-598b-98d3-80141362cf9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308922} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef3f142-894c-5b10-aa8a-eec2f6e84223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308923} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6a0d7e-80ec-5f26-9f7f-259f3ccc9785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308924} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc8af7d-10b8-574c-b1a9-ffc6d88c0f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1576000c-cf8b-598b-98d3-80141362cf9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308922} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5c5251-bf49-502a-89e9-a370b6dea870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6579b20d-d77a-52e5-a1dc-300255db1f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-97352" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ k \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot ((-4)\\cdot (-3))+k\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =48+2k+0 \\\\  & =48+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 48+2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-48 \\\\ k & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bb43030-21b9-555b-be6d-63deddeb5ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308928} {:answer/id #uuid "b7befcde-8599-5308-bf7f-937e32572d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308929} {:answer/id #uuid "a800c079-4c2c-554d-a222-13834b7ece71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308930} {:answer/id #uuid "289bc7a8-f7c9-57d7-bef9-3b9654c13d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308931} {:answer/id #uuid "06d1bd45-86b6-5055-96b8-b707abe5369f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a800c079-4c2c-554d-a222-13834b7ece71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308930} :answer-field/id #uuid "702df06a-b5d6-56cb-aab9-b2061eead34b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00a694c6-6f0f-5c3c-9a1a-e19c0a66499c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97354" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =2{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =2(k\\cdot (-3))+5\\cdot (-3\\cdot 2)+3\\cdot (4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-6k-30+36 \\\\  & =6-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6-6k & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a03d53-6e3c-5d7e-b39b-ef4ddd32654f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308935} {:answer/id #uuid "6aceb5ac-2608-5a7f-b4ec-4a352dfcb762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308936} {:answer/id #uuid "01084dae-567d-5a25-ab59-50bf4cc3bca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308937} {:answer/id #uuid "07b25c68-780b-5e2d-adac-9ffe36983d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308938} {:answer/id #uuid "90426f37-ed93-5189-87cc-830edda9ee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01084dae-567d-5a25-ab59-50bf4cc3bca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308937} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b6a2b1-f5ee-532b-aadb-ffde37c868e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "131bc7fb-6fc9-5e64-83f4-c7089c6d2d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-97355" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-4)y(-4)+x(2)y(2)+x(4)y(4).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k-2t$ and $q(t)=5-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-4)q(-4)+p(2)q(2)+p(4)q(4) \\\\  & =(k+8)(5-8)+(k-4)(5-2)+(k-8)(5-8) \\\\  & =(-3k-24)+(3k-12)+(-3k+24) \\\\  & =-3k-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -3k-12 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =-12 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e33b4a8c-77fa-56dd-8c7b-456c7e0a48c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133287} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6d1e57-a43b-5d0f-8162-7455cfa8f3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133288} {:answer/id #uuid "41f66508-2a3c-5abf-a611-61cc16dc4e50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133289} {:answer/id #uuid "47d4776d-5d34-5597-96ab-78d5855743e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133290} {:answer/id #uuid "7775ecb0-2c2c-5a3b-bb7b-4ae913ff42aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d4776d-5d34-5597-96ab-78d5855743e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133290} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b13418-ad01-5ca2-a69b-54bc675df494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6628de02-57af-5dca-abf1-406df4d71a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97359" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k{t}^{2}-2t$ and $q(t)=2t+1$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(1)q(1)+p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(k-2)(2+1)+(0)(0+1)+(k+2)(-2+1) \\\\  & =(3k-6)+0+(-k-2) \\\\  & =2k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ee6f458-059f-5089-8b01-9a1e6f548462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308942} {:answer/id #uuid "5a11b1ae-fada-5005-bd66-ff5c77d9a95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308943} {:answer/id #uuid "6b30ded1-ec87-5f36-b1fa-c3e4adb378ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308944} {:answer/id #uuid "029a292e-c0c8-5769-a8df-b8750573e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308945} {:answer/id #uuid "69b702c4-fc17-56da-87b5-95928e09085c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029a292e-c0c8-5769-a8df-b8750573e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308945} :answer-field/id #uuid "d741525d-6f40-5084-86dd-75dd3f0c4421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb77c4e3-9a2f-52aa-a423-e70fc36097a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97361" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-2)y(-2)+x(0)y(0)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=1-2t$ and $q(t)=2-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-2)q(-2)+p(0)q(0)+p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(1+4)(2-4k)+(1)(2)+(1-4)(2-4k) \\\\  & =(10-20k)+2+(-6+12k) \\\\  & =6-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ecc6e12-1139-58f2-bf90-7991496394dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308949} {:answer/id #uuid "3922ddfa-ee7c-505b-9222-e46fbdf3e612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308950} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8c0217-3d59-54b8-8490-289a1b0c33b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308951} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa04f4a-1207-54e9-bbb7-ad14ac1ed0b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308952} {:answer/id #uuid "805827ac-b29a-5e3d-b45c-b3552a596107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fa04f4a-1207-54e9-bbb7-ad14ac1ed0b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308952} :answer-field/id #uuid "54046064-2fe2-5840-b404-7962030586a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4af250cc-437f-50b7-9f23-d84529f8aaae" :question/math-academy-id "q-97438" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-k$ and $y(t)=-2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-k)(-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2k-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2kt+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\pi k+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\pi k-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi k-2 & =0 \\\\ \\pi k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6fafbc-3524-5f64-b459-3698df8b35ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308956} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4d7335-3ff1-55ee-988f-ecf05889acab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308957} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bb1ca9-dd2a-5e8b-bbb0-473210b7a8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308958} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7789c0-1483-5818-b458-87586c50ba3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308959} {:answer/id #uuid "77723d8b-97e0-5ecf-a784-4013011ecd3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6fafbc-3524-5f64-b459-3698df8b35ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308956} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b75cfa-37eb-56b3-9b86-260ea837f3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "579248ee-641f-5ef7-a9f1-c830a24589fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97440" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=5{t}^{2}-2$ and $y(t)=k+3{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-2)(k+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{4}+(5k-6){t}^{2}-2k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{t}^{5}+\\frac{(5k-6){t}^{3}}{3}-2kt){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k & =0 \\\\ 9+(5k-6)-6k & =0 \\\\ 3-k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7405b6e-64f3-56f5-a888-dc51c2b76b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308963} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9562eb-29d4-5193-8cf2-e85cf472728b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308964} {:answer/id #uuid "1caf3857-d930-5c07-8600-16e177a50c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308965} {:answer/id #uuid "804a6404-ba64-5a54-965e-0a1acfe946be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308966} {:answer/id #uuid "59c618ed-d097-5cdb-8300-ac5abc28b7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c9562eb-29d4-5193-8cf2-e85cf472728b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308964} :answer-field/id #uuid "a256e3a6-51b7-519b-be95-c3ae1a530343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12ad231b-cf68-5ed9-b5d4-80776b593537" :question/math-academy-id "q-97443" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1))-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67cd2901-db7d-5a1f-ae27-2ba0b38c96a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308970} {:answer/id #uuid "9c34072c-5988-5515-a7ce-d328e36106f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308971} {:answer/id #uuid "e587b8c6-83f9-5d79-b369-b7791276bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308972} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a2d029-3159-577d-9e55-4e3451b7c4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308973} {:answer/id #uuid "f64bcfd0-22b7-5d04-b678-faecdf9a7255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e587b8c6-83f9-5d79-b369-b7791276bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308972} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6030c2d-9773-598e-b8be-79fa3d4c9985" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6302273c-fd2b-591e-9cb8-0335b24472a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97445" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=1$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f71e54e-252d-5726-8698-4718580455c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308977} {:answer/id #uuid "163720aa-1ae1-55ac-9641-3f19a6086cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308978} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c193f2-7858-563f-a1e2-43c8a64684c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308979} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd3ace4-4843-5002-91dd-e9145fb40aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308980} {:answer/id #uuid "69ccfb07-7cd2-531a-856b-b46a797ebecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecd3ace4-4843-5002-91dd-e9145fb40aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308980} :answer-field/id #uuid "b655d98e-6382-5e55-8c7b-7a75d4fe603c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fd2aa21-7f1e-565a-9442-874ab5688a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-97450" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
